{"question": "Cause of apical periodontitis is/are:", "options": {"A": "Sequel of pulpal diseases", "B": "Wedging of foreign object between the teeth", "C": "High points in restoration", "D": "All of the above"}, "pred_answer": "A", "gold_answer": "A", "question_domains": ["Dentistry", "Oral Medicine", "Endodontics", "Pathology", "Infectious Diseases"], "option_domains": ["Dentistry", "Oral Surgery"], "question_analyses": {"Dentistry": "Apical periodontitis is an inflammatory condition that affects the tissues surrounding the apex (tip) of a tooth's root. This condition is typically a result of an infection or inflammation that originates from within the tooth, often due to untreated dental caries (cavities) or trauma. The infection can spread from the pulp (the innermost part of the tooth containing nerves and blood vessels) to the surrounding bone and tissues, leading to apical periodontitis. The causes of apical periodontitis include:\n\n1. **Untreated Dental Caries**: When tooth decay reaches the pulp, it can cause infection and inflammation, leading to apical periodontitis. 2. **Trauma**: Physical injury to the tooth can damage the pulp, leading to inflammation and infection. 3. **Cracked Tooth**: A crack in the tooth can expose the pulp to bacteria, leading to infection. 4. **Failed Root Canal Treatment**: If a root canal treatment is not completed properly, it can leave residual infection that can cause apical periodontitis. 5. **Recurrent Caries**: If decay occurs around a previously treated tooth, it can lead to reinfection of the pulp. 6. **Periodontal Disease**: In some cases, periodontal disease can lead to an infection that affects the tooth's root apex..", "Oral Medicine": "The condition being depicted in the question is apical periodontitis, which is an inflammatory process that occurs in the periapical tissues (the tissues surrounding the apex of a tooth root) as a result of infection or inflammation originating from the dental pulp. This condition i